==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|regent}}, from {{der|en|xno|regent}}, {{der|en|frm|regent}}, and their source, {{der|en|la|regēns|t=ruling; ruler, governor, prince}}, present participle of {{m|la|regō|t=I govern, I steer}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹiːd͡ʒənt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-regent.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} A [[ruler]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# One who [[rule]]s in place of the [[monarch]], especially because the monarch is too [[young]], [[absent]], or [[disable]]d. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|_|historical}} A member of a [[municipal]] or [[civic]] body of [[governors]], especially in certain European cities. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=Geert Mak|translator=w:Philipp Blom|title=Amsterdam: A Brief Life of the City|page=139|publisher=Vintage|year_published=2001
|passage=This perception, however, does no justice to the '''regents''' of the city of Amsterdam.}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland|North America}} A member of governing board of a [[college]] or [[university]]; also a governor of the Smithsonian Institute in Washington DC. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
# {{lb|en|Indonesia}} The chief executive of a [[regency]]

